# Mental Health Law

- **LOs:**
  - Mental health ordinance of Hong Kong
  - Compulsory admission
  - Mental capacity
  - How to find out best interest
- **CAP 136 Mental Healh Ordinance:**
  - Evolved from Asylums ordinance enacted in 1936, and subsequently consolidated in 1950 into Mental Hospital Ordiance
  - 1960 Mental Hospital Ordinance repealed following enactment of Mental Health Ordinance which is more elaborate (5 parts) (substantial amendments were during 1996-1997, 5 Parts into 10 Sections)
  - Core issue balance <u>between control and care</u>
- **Compulsory admission and treatment** (Part III Reception, Detention, and Treatment in MHO):
  - <u>Gazette ward</u> - (1) chief executive gave order declare anyware which is property of the HKGov to be a mental hospital for detention, custody andc are of mentally disordered persons (such order shall be published in the Gazette)
  - Non gazette psychiatric wards are just informal wards and are free to leave the wards
  - In fact admission to such wards can be compulsory or voluntary
  - Relevant sections ss.30 (voluntary), 31, 32, and 36 (compulsion)
  - **\< 16y and parents or guardian:**
    - Desirious of such person should receive Tx
    - Completes an aplication
    - Lodges the application with a medical superintendent
  - **Voluntary patient for discharge ?DAMA** - assumed to be mentally capable (although not mentioned in ss. 30), and aware of:
    - His rights (how to make a request of discharge in writing)
    - His duties (e.g. compliance w/ Tx)
  - **Compulsory admission** (initiatiation reception):
    - **Form 1:**
      - Relative makes the appplication
      - Approved social worker or medical practitioner seen patient in 14d (ss 35A) if relative not available
      - Application made on grounds (ss 31(1)) suffering mental disorder of nature or degree, ought to be detained in interest of <u>own health or safety</u> OR <u>with a view to protection of other people</u>
    - **Form 2:**
      - Made by registered medical practioner that has examined patient within \< 7d (usu. A&E)
    - **Form 3** - migistrate submits form 3, patient can request to see district court judge
    - **Form 4 and form 7** - happens in psychiatry ward (form 4 extension to 21d, form 7 detention until mental health review tribunal)
- **Conditional discharge** (2 years ago suggested making this change):
  - Applied Hx of criminal violence or disposition to commit such violence
  - For those liable to be detained (not for those voluntary previously, but 2y ago suggest making this change, but no progress)
  - Conditional discharge where conditions include:
    - Reside
    - Attend OPD
    - Take medications
    - To be under supervision of Director of Social Welfare (MSW visits in practice)
- **Mental capacity:**
  - Mental incapacity and mentally incapacitated person defined in section 2 in MHO (MIP also defined elsewhere)
  - Mental incapacity - Mental disorder, mental handicap
  - MIP - two different definitions:
    - Unable to managing and administering his property and affairs (Part II Managing properties and affairs)
    - Mentally handicap (in all other context of MHO
  - Mental disorder - defined in section 2:
    - Mental illness
    - State of arrested or incomplete development of mind which is associated w/ abnormally aggresive or seriously irresponible conduct
    - Psychopathic disorder (personality disorder, resulting in <u>aggressive or seriously irresponsible conduct</u>
    - Disability of minds
  - Mental handicap - subaverage general intelelctual function w/ deficenesi in adaptive behaviour
  - Limitation –\> theoretically context specific and task specific, which is not seen in the definitions above
  - Mental capacity:
    - Incapable of giving consent if that person is incapable of understanding the general and nature and effect of the Tx
  - MacCAT-T - clinical tool for assessment of consent Tx (see slide 63-64)
  - If patient is capable of asking relevant questions, this is a robust piece of evidence of mental capacity
  - Concept of proportionality - capacity required proportional to severity of condition (allows autonomy and best interest)
- Best interest balances between:
  - Right of adult who is mentally incapabiliy to receive medical and dental treatment needed
  - The right of such persons to be ssafeguarded from improper medical and dental treatment

# Updates on antidepressants, Mood Stabilising Agents, Anxiolytics

## Anxiolytics

- **Classes:**
  - Benzodiazepine
  - Anti-convulsants (pregabalin)
- **Benzodiazepine:**
  - <u>MOA</u> - GABA-A BDZ receptor complex:
    - Enhances the actions of GABA, but still requires presence of GABA
    - Enhances Cl influx for inhibitory effect
    - Not all GABA-A receptors show affinity to BDZ (80% have high affinity)
  - High affinity group subdivided by their affinity to zolpidem (a hypnotics, ?Zolpidem):
    - BDZ type 1 - high affinity to zolpidem (omega subunit)
    - BDZ type II - low or no affinity to zolpidem
  - No GABA –\> benzodiazepine unlikely to have effect
  - <u>See types and pharmacokinetics</u> (slide 11):
    - Diazepam fastest acting w/ longest half life over lorazepam, but longest half life means S/E will be more prolonged
    - Active metabolite low concentration (Alprazolam \[Zanex\]) maybe useful to reduce rebound anxiety
    - Lorazepam only phase II metabolism, less hepatotoxic
    - Zolpidem vs zolpiclone probably main difference between active metabolite… if induction insomnia (zolpidem), if difficult to maintain sleep (zolpiclone for active metabolite effect)
  - <u>Clinical use in psychiatry of BZD</u>:
    - Anxiety disorders (N.B. only Sx relief initially for 4 weeks due to tolerance, AD for maintenance)
    - Sleep disorders
    - Alcohol withdrawal
    - Epilepsy
    - Agitation/ restlessness (beware paradoxical reaction due esp w/ disinhibition, e.g. intelectual disability)
  - <u>S/E:</u>
    - Oversedation - drowsiness, dizziness, psychomotor impairment, withdrawal
    - Other somatic Sx:
      - Blurred vision, GI upset, headache, respiratory depression
      - Falls in elderly
    - Other rare AE:
      - Amnesia
      - Paradoxical reaction
      - Respiratory arest
      - Convulsions on withdrawal
      - Drug of abuse/ dependence
      - Intoxication
      - Withdrawal (paranoid ideas)
  - <u>Know the benzos/ Z drugs of abuse and their names</u> (see phone for pictures):
    - Midazolam - blue, cross
    - Nimetazepam - Fi
    - Zolpidem/ zolpiclone - white gua zi
  - <u>Considerations</u>:
    - Longer half-life in patient to prevent double withdrawal
    - In outpatient depends on setting
- **Anticonvulsants** (Pregabalin):
  - <u>MOA</u> - structurally like GABA but does not actually bind to GABA receptors:
    - Voltage-gated callcium channel in CNS
  - <u>Clinical indications</u>:
    - GAD (off label use for other anxiety disorders)
    - Neuropathic pain
    - ASM (partial-onset seizure)
    - Fibromyalgia
  - Pregabalin can be abused under current understanding

## Antidepressants

- **Neuromechanisms of depression:**
  - Monoamine hypothesis
  - HPA axis
  - Neural plasticity
  - Neuroinflammation
  - Neurodegeneration
- **Classes** - all work on monoamine hypothesis:
  - SSRI
  - NDRI
  - MAOI
  - TCA
- **New classses:**
  - NMDA receptor antagonist
  - Serotonin partial agonist receptor uptake inhibitor/ antagonist receptor uptake inhibitor (SPaRI/ SPaARI)
  - GABA A alosteric modulator {GABAAAM)
- **Esketamine:**
  - Enantiomer of racemic ketamine
  - FDA approved Tx as augmentation medication (i.e. adjuvant) in Rx-resistant depression (defined as failed 2 trials of licensed medications at adjuvant dose and duration \[6-8 weeks\])
  - <u>ROA</u> - nasal spray device
  - <u>MOA</u> - ?neuroplasticity so increased synaptic connection to increase monoamine pathway through SSRI:
    - Non-competitiva N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor antagonist removes inhibitory effect in glutamate release, release BDNF
    - Effects on synaptogenesis (impaired synaptic connection implicated in MDD, resulting in Tx resistance)
    - GLutamate established role in regulating synaptic connections (glutamate misregulation possibly implicated in MDD?)
  - <u>Dosing in slides</u> - varying dose with time
  - <u>Potential for abuse</u>:
    - Observed Tx to prevent patients from taking home
- **Tineptine:**
  - <u>MOA</u>:
    - Serotonin dependence
    - NMDA inhibitor
    - Neuro-opioid receptor (but S/E, resulting in withdrawal)
- **Dextromethorphan and bupropion** (Auvelity):
  - Dextromethorphan as cough suppressant, inhibit 5HT and NA transporter (?SNRI). and reversible blocker of NMDA type receptor
  - Bupropion ER is an antidepressants (NDRI)
- **Zurzuzvae** / Zuranolone (post-partum depression):
  - Neuroactive steroid (non-specific effect on many CNS receptors) for HPA hypothesis
  - GABA-A alosteric modulator
  - Can only take for 14 days
  - S/E CNS depressants
- Clinical uses (see slides)
- Take notes on NMS and serotonin syndrome
- Suicidality increased in \< 25y after antidepressants based on age-stratified study
- See the meta-analysis:
  - Fluoxetine vs agomelatine stands out in terms of tolerability
- Consider 6 mo for drug withdrawal

## Mood Stablizing Agents

- **Classification based on:**
  - Acute Tx
  - Maintenance Tx
- Epilim (valproate) a/w neurodevelopment disorder in offspring

# Genes, Brain, and Psychiatry

- **Mind-centred psychopathology** (cf descriptive) - mid 20th centuries:
  - General psychopathology (Jasper) states brain mythology, and emphasizes subjective mental phenomena
  - Brain mythology (mental disorders occur w/o organic brain disease)
- **Mind-centric psychiatry:**
  - Built on Jasper
  - Subjective mental phenomena
  - sfunctional defense mechanisms
  - Psychotherapeutic interactions
  - However such theories move away from biological basis (Rosenhan 1973 famous study regarding over-diagnosis)
  - ?social constructs to justify compulsory detention and Tx
  - Psychiatry and anti-psychiatry movements arise from such, subsequently return to the brain in second half of 20th century, i.e. biological psychiatry in models of mental disorders
- **Neuropsychiatry** - subspecialty for those w/ documented brain pathology/ focal neurological S/S
- **Biological psychiatry** - triggered by:
  - Psychopharmacology (e.g. chlorpromazine effective for schizophrenia and mania)
  - Genetics (twin studies demonstrate genetic and therefore biological basis)
  - Neuroimaging
- **Neuroanatomy:**
- **Sensory input to brainstem:**
  - Many sensory modalities relayed to brainstem
  - Relatively crude but important in signifying important objects or events (salience)
- **Internal state and hypothalamus:**
  - Homeostasis
  - Other physiological processes, through ANS, hormones, brainstem
  - Eating disorders and sleep disorders
- **Monoamine arousal system** - know 3 systems and functions and the site of neurotransmitter production:
  - <u>Norad and serotonin</u> - diffuse generalised projection
  - <u>Dopa</u> - projection to frontal part of brain, modulate/ prioritise and initiate behaviour ?in response to salience
- **Regions for fear/ anxiety/ aggression:**
  - Miebrain periaquedductal gray stimulated from innately fearful stimuli, impede ascending pain signals and activate innate defense behaviour
  - Amygdala from associative learning (fear learning), basolateral amygdala for learning and central amygdala for expression, triggers SNS activation
  - Hypothalamus mediates humoral stress response
  - Limbic and prefrontal cortex, mediates conscious component of fear and anxiety
- **Neurobiology of depression** - acute vs chronic stress (social signal transduction theory of depression):
  - Acute stress –\> mainly activated HPA axis, dampen inflammation
  - Chronic stress - glucocorticoid insensitivity, monoamine depletion, ?neuroinflammation and plasticity
- **Basal ganglia:**
- **Cerebellum:**
- **Anterior cingulate gyrus and insula:**
- **Pre-frontal cortex:**
  - Dorolateral prefrontal cortex - working memory/ reasoning (part of Central Control Network)
  - Ventromedial - social cognition and behaviour (Default Mode Network)
- **Dopamine-cortical interactions:**
  - Meso-cortical dopaminergic pathway activates when salience stimulus (danger/ reward)
  - Because invariably activated in psychosis, i.e. eberrant salience causes confusion of the pre-frontal cortex, which results in construction of delusions to explain the new reality where everything is salient (accounts for loosening of association, ?thought railing, formal thought disorder, ambivalence)

# Psychotherapy

- **Definition** - the relief of distress or disability in one person by another, using an approach based on a particular theory or paradigm, and a requirement that the agent performing the therapy has had some form of training in delivering this. It is these latter two points which distinguish psychotherapy from other forms of counseling or caregiving
- **Theories:**
  - Framework for asking, observing, and response, intervene, and assess progress
  - No best, rather plethora of viable theories from which to choose from
- **Forms:**
  - Individual
  - Coupe
  - Family and Group
- **Major schools:**
  - Psychoanalysis \> 3x/w (or broader psychodynamic psychotherapy which is less intense)
  - CBT
  - Newer shchools - IBT, mindfulness, DBT etc.
- **4 major applications of psychoanalysis:**
  - Theory of how the mind works
  - Treatment methodology
  - Research
  - View of culture or social phenomenon
- **Works on histeria:**
  - Hysterical Sx w/ meaning simultaneously hidden and revealed
  - All neurotic Sx are repressed unconsciousness psychic contents
  - Initially revealed through hypnosis, later in life free association and psychoanalysis (talking cure)
  - Sexual and death drives
- **Early childhood experiences:**
  - Fantasies not met in childhod in conflict with reality repressed and becomes unconsciousness
- **Oedipus complex:**
  - Increased aiwareness of the sexual nature of the parents' relationship from which they are excluded
  - Child thinks that because the mother takes care 24/7 (limited by Child's cognitive capability), but realise that mother life is much more complex
  - Jealousy against father, siblings, other important relationships in child's and mother's life
  - Super-ego development
- **Freud's Hierarchy of Defense mechanisms:**
  - Primative defense mechanism (borderline/ antisocial personalities tend to do this)
  - Neurotic defense mechanism (more mature personalities)
  - Mature defense mechanism
- **Dreams as wish fulfilment:**
  - Fulfillment of infatile libidinal desires
  - In disguise, requires analysis, i.e. the royal road to the unconsciousness
- **Transference and counter-transference:**
  - Unconscious templating based on past experience to navigate everyday life
  - Tendency of mind to view and identify new situations from the templates and earlier experiences
  - Transference occurs when patient re-expereince major infantile conflicts or traumas, and patient may view the analyst like a parental figure, as if within the original child-parent relationship
  - Counter-transference as an obstacle, how patient stirs up past memories in the therapies
  - Requires undergoing personal analysis to keep counter-transference in check
- **Topographic model vs structural model:** 
![](../img/freudian_topographic_model_vs_structural_model.png)
- **Jung:**
  - Archetypes, collective unconsciousness
  - Libido not as sexual but generalised life energy, alone cannot explain everything
- **Melanie Klein** - child psychoanalyst:
  - Play as a substitute of Freudian free association
  - Infant mode of organizing experiences can be categorized into two:
    - Paranoid-schizoid position - good/bad breast, two different individuals
    - Depressive position - realized both reflecting the same person, consider harming the monther, and thought to loved objects lead to depressive anxiety and starts to experience guilt
- **Object relations** - extend from primarily a inner world, to relations to object for socio-emotional development:
  - Wilfred Bion
  - John Bowlby (Stranger situation study - secure vs insecure attachment \[avoidant, ambivalent, disorganised\])
  - Donald Winnicott
- **Self-Psychology** (Heiz Kohut)
- Key interventions in psychoanalysis
- **3 main phases of psychoanalysis:**
  - Rapport
  - Transference, interpretation and resolution
  - Termination
- **Developed from psychoanalytic method:**
  - Psychodrama
  - Couples- and Family-
  - Group
  - New focuses include cost-effectiveness, evidence based medicine, mannuelised
- **Manulised psychodynamic therapy:**
  - Mentalisation-based therapy
  - Panic-focused Psycho-analytic psychotherapy
  - Transference-focused psychotherapy
  - Dynamic interpersonal therapy
- **Cognitive behaviour therapy:**
  - Based on behaviourism and learning theory
  - Systematic desensitisation
  - Cognitve componant added as:
    - Humans do not respond to stimuli but a mental representaiton of the stimuli
    - HUman learning is more cognitively mediated
    - Maladaptive cognition central to psychopathology
  - Core concepts:
  - ABC model - activating events, beliefs, and consequence for
  - 3 levels of cognition - usually developed in a step wise manner:
    - Core beliefs
    - Dysfunctional asumptions
    - Automatism
  - 6 phases:

# Clinical Skills Section: Child Psychiatry

- **Special domains in C&A psychiatry:**
  - Developmental perspective
  - Family dynamics and parenting styles
  - School involvement
- **More structured assessment:**
- **How different interviewing an adult vs C&A:**
  - Individual vs joint interview
  - Free play and Sx observation
  - Limit settings (rules for the kids; e.g. no electronic devices as limits assessment)
  - Use drawing and playing
  - Obseving parenting & child-parent relationships
  - Special tasks
- **Special tasks:**
  - 3-step tasks (chinese, english writing tasks, drawing)
  - TOM tests
  - 3 wishes (indirect assessment of insight, dependent on context)
  - Future aspirations
- **Personal Hx:**
  - Birth Hx - antenatal, delivery, neonatal period
  - Developemental milestone
  - Schools by sections (teacher complaint, adaptation, peer relationships, academic performance, ECA)
  - Work experience
- **Temperemant** - cf personality yet to completely development:
  - Prevailing mood, coping, leisure & hobbies, peer relationships
  - Dating experiences, relevant psychosexual Hx
  - Religion
- **Family background:**
  - Parents/ carers - demographic, health, personality (temper, coping style, hobbies, ASD/ADHD), discipline and parenting
  - Parents marital relation
  - Siblings
  - FHx of mental illness
- **P/E:**
  - Syndromal features
  - Child abuse
  - Self harm
  - Eating disorders (e.g. Russell's knuckles)
- **Principles of assessing ADHD:**
  - Requires clarification, not just a checklist
  - Pervasive, occurs in all settings, although possibly fluctuating
- **Attention span:**
  - Young kids - age x2-3
  - lower primary - 15-20 min
  - secondary school - 30-40 min
  - university - 45 min-1h
- Mx:
  - Pharmacological (amphetamines)
  - Classroom interventions
  - CBT
- **ASD:**
  - Social deficits
  - Communication deficits
  - Restrictive/ repetitive patterns
